  "coverage": {
    "dateOfInjuryFrom": "2010-01-01",
    "dateOfInjuryTo": "2027-01-01",
    "belowCoverageReason": "DIR's current benefits publication tabulates dates of injury from 2010-01-01 onward. Earlier eras require the archived DIR tables or the dated text of Lab. Code § 4453 (KB-150), neither of which is ingested yet.",
    "atOrAboveCoverageReason": "Lab. Code § 4453(a)(10) adjusts the limits every January 1, so this snapshot expires. The 2027-01-01 figures were not published when the snapshot was taken, and serving the prior era in their place would be a fabricated answer."
  },
  "citations": {
    "temporaryDisability": [
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4453(a) (minimum and maximum average weekly earnings, by date of injury)",
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4453(a)(10) (state-average-weekly-wage indexing, injuries on or after 2006-01-01)",
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4653 (temporary total disability at two-thirds of average weekly earnings)",
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4661.5 (payment made two years or more after date of injury)"
    ],
    "permanentDisability": [
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4453(b) (average weekly earnings for permanent partial disability)",
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4658 (permanent disability weeks by rating)",
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4659 (permanent total disability; life pension)"
    ],
    "supplementalJobDisplacement": [
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4658.5 (voucher, injuries 2004-01-01 through 2012-12-31)",
      "Cal. Lab. Code § 4658.7 (voucher, injuries on or after 2013-01-01)"
    ]
  },
  "reviewStatus": "unverified",
  "reviewNotes": [
    "UNVERIFIED. Every figure and citation is unverified professional data until counsel confirms it. Figures marked `dir_published` were printed by DIR in the snapshot named in `source`; that is provenance, not legal verification.",
    "DERIVED MAXIMA. DIR printed an explicit temporary-disability maximum only through the 1/1/12 era. From 1/1/13 onward the maximum is derived as round(2/3 x maximum average weekly earnings, 2), per Lab. Code § 4653. The derivation reproduces all 3 maxima DIR did print and all 17 minima DIR prints, which is evidence the rule is right — not proof that it is the rule counsel would apply.",
    "DATE OF INJURY IS THE ANCHOR, WITH ONE EXCEPTION. These windows are keyed to date of injury. Lab. Code § 4661.5 makes a temporary total disability payment made two years or more after the date of injury turn on the rate in effect on the PAYMENT date instead. That exception is modelled in `benefit-rate-anchors.ts`, not here.",
    "CUMULATIVE TRAUMA UNRESOLVED. \"Date of injury\" is itself a legal construct for cumulative trauma (Lab. Code § 5412). This dataset takes a date; it does not decide what that date is.",
    "PD 100% IS OUT OF RANGE BY DESIGN. DIR publishes permanent-disability bands up to 99 percent. A 100 percent rating is permanent TOTAL disability, paid at the temporary-disability rate for life under Lab. Code § 4659, and is deliberately not answerable from the PD bands.",
    "THE § 4661.5 FLOOR PROVISO IS NOT MODELLED. § 4661.5 excludes the payment-date rate when it produces a LOWER payment because of a reduction in the minimum average weekly earnings. That is a computation concern, not a rate-selection concern; only the maximum is exposed under the delayed rule, so the unmodelled floor cannot be silently applied."
  ],
